Distinguish rate evidence from mechanism transport and instrument response (@chemistry-kinetic-evidence: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Connect-chemical-composition-to-a-signal-and-expose-missing-information.lingua#r_0E739CDF730C0B949DBB0338EF") { r_061D260150F0C26CE4B3280AA5
A rate law is an inferred or stipulated relation under specified conditions. An overall balanced equation constrains constituent accounting; it does not generally determine reaction orders. Reuse [Define a reaction rate and infer a law from appropriate evidence](../general-chemistry/kinetics/Reaction-rates-and-rate-laws.lingua#r_2RAAQPESP3HCEDY95PCFTXVVYZ), [Define the units and comparisons before choosing an allocation](../../inquiry/core/Define-the-units-and-comparisons-before-choosing-an-allocation.lingua#r_0D6650DB3035D66F497D72ADE2), and the sampling/comparison principles already developed there. Changes in temperature, mixing, preparation or observation timing can confound a comparison attributed only to concentration.

An original initial-rate table at one stated temperature uses concentrations in mmol/L and rates in mmol L⁻¹ min⁻¹. Rows (c_A,c_B,r) are (1,1,0.10), (2,1,0.40) and (1,2,0.20). Within a proposed power-law family, doubling A multiplies the rate by four and doubling B multiplies it by two, consistent with r=k c_A²c_B and k=0.10 L² mmol⁻² min⁻¹. Three exact stipulated rows do not prove that this family is globally correct. Infinitely many functions can coincide at those rows. Choose a held-out concentration and explain what a conflicting observation would refute.

In a constant-volume disappearance model, slow supply of A to a reactive region can limit the observed loss even if reaction there is intrinsically rapid. A bulk concentration need not equal the local concentration in the rate law. Conversely, an instrument with finite response time can produce a changing indication after the underlying composition has stopped changing. A first-order-looking trace can arise from either process. Reuse [Distinguish chemical rate from mixing, diffusion, and heat-transfer effects](../general-chemistry/kinetics/Reaction-versus-transport.lingua#r_72WYPRVE8CS893QJVFB49FZ9HE) contextually for the later reaction/transport analysis, without requiring its full continuum formation at this common step.

Changes in mixing, sample geometry or sensor response can help distinguish competing explanations, but the change may affect more than one variable. Preserve an explicit comparison design and independent observations. Repeated identical biased measurements reduce neither every systematic effect nor structural ambiguity. An activation-energy diagram, a heat release and a kinetic trace supply different information; a lower apparent barrier alone is not a complete account of a multistep catalytic system.

**Read and criticize:** [Chemistry 2e — Flowers, Theopold, Langley, and Robinson](../general-chemistry/sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94), sections 12.3 and 12.7, with the qualifications above; the original shared mechanisms and transport continuations remain later depth. Reproduce the table’s ratios and units, construct a second positive rate function agreeing at all three rows, and propose a comparison where it differs. Write competing chemical, transport and sensor explanations for one qualitative decay trace, identifying the evidence needed to separate them. Do not claim a mechanism from a fit alone.

